-- Step BB-2: the single garden door templates, and FD7's.
-- Run by Nathan in the Supabase SQL editor. Safe to re-run.
-- Run AFTER sql/step-bb1 (and after the Step AZ and BA files, which are live).
--
-- Nathan (13 Oct 2026): single garden doors are sold plain, with a fanlight
-- over, with full-height side light(s), and with half-height flag lights made
-- as SEPARATE coupled frames. This file is the ONE-FRAME layouts; coupled
-- frames are the next step.
--
-- Five templates, group "Open Out Doors", family `door`, profile
-- `open_out_doors`, product door, window_type "French Door" where there is a
-- pair and "Door" where there is a single leaf:
--
--    28  "Doorset with Fully Glazed Sidelights"  2820 x 2199
--          -> "French Doors With Glazed Side Panels"              sort 45
--    15  "Fully Glazed Single Door"              1099 x 2199
--          -> "Single Door"                                       sort 60
--        (built on the board from 15, Integrate has no template:)
--          -> "Single Door With Fanlight"        1099 x 2599      sort 70
--          -> "Single Door With Side Light"      1699 x 2199      sort 80
--          -> "Single Door With Side Lights And Fanlights"
--                                                2249 x 2599      sort 90
--
-- Each tree was GENERATED by the board's own code (buildSingleDoorFrame and
-- buildFrenchDoorSidePanelFrame), so each template is exactly what the board
-- would build and assertSaveable passes on it.
--
-- WHAT THE BOARD CHOSE, and where it departs from the drawings that were read:
--
--  * a FANLIGHT takes Nathan's 62 bottom rail (difference 5), a SIDE light
--    keeps Integrate's 86 — which is what its own drawings have and what the
--    SD2 / SD4 benchmarks price. On these templates that moves the fanlight
--    rails from the Utile63x96 section to Utile63x63 (difference 7b);
--  * the door keeps its own 1005 column (template 15's internal width) and
--    the side lights share what is left, equally. SD3's single side light is
--    therefore 559 wide and SD4's two are 534 each, which are Integrate's own
--    figures;
--  * template 28's side columns are EQUAL (619 / 1406 / 619) where
--    Integrate's are 618 / 1405 / 621, as every GlazePro doorset is;
--  * its two glazed panels both take 12 mm hidden in the rebate. Integrate's
--    own A1 stores 0 — the same template quirk as FD5's A1 — which the FD7
--    FIXTURE keeps because a fixture's job is Integrate's figures;
--  * a single door leaf is NEVER arched. Integrate's board hands a dropped-in
--    leaf Arch Head and Half Circle switched on (archHeight 389.5), which
--    priced SD2 as 1.14 m2 of shaped glass and 240 minutes of swept-head work
--    until the reviewer unticked it.
--
-- NO stored ironmongery list and NO stored surround list, as every GlazePro
-- template since Step AX. Integrate's own default rules give a single door
-- exactly the list Nathan wants — Hardwick Lyndon espag set, two 102 mm hinge
-- pairs, a double euro cylinder, the slot vent and the grille — which costs
-- 59.85 more than the template's stored Windsor list (differences 9 and 10).
--
-- ALSO IN THIS FILE, A TIDY: the two Step AZ templates were created with
-- window_type "Door" and the three Step BA ones with "French Door". They are
-- all French doors, so the two AZ rows are corrected. Drawings already made
-- keep whatever they were created with — this touches templates only.
--
-- Product and Quantity are both is_required (Step AQ), so the product code is
-- looked up BY LABEL and the script fails loudly rather than writing a code
-- that would look set and still fail the check.
--
-- RUN SECTION 0 FIRST.

-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 0 — read before changing
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

-- 0a. Every door template as it stands, with its window_type — the tidy
--     below changes the two "Door" rows that hold a French pair.
SELECT id, name, family, group_name, window_type, sort_order, is_active
FROM   public.drawing_templates
WHERE  family = 'door'
ORDER  BY sort_order, name;

-- 0b. Do any of this step's five names already exist? A re-run updates them
--     in place; anything else here is somebody else's template.
SELECT id, name, sort_order, is_active, updated_at
FROM   public.drawing_templates
WHERE  name IN (
    'French Doors With Glazed Side Panels',
    'Single Door',
    'Single Door With Fanlight',
    'Single Door With Side Light',
    'Single Door With Side Lights And Fanlights'
)
ORDER  BY name;

-- 0c. The containment sql/step-bb1 adds. Expect the frame pair to be there;
--     if it is not, run step-bb1 first — the DO block below refuses anyway.
SELECT parent_code, child_code, min_count, max_count
FROM   part_type_children
WHERE  (parent_code = 'assemblyFramePart' AND child_code = 'doorLeafPart')
   OR  (parent_code = 'doorLeafPart')
ORDER  BY parent_code, child_code;

-- 0d. The product codes, so the lookup below can be checked by eye.
SELECT code, label, sort_order, is_active
FROM   reference_options
WHERE  category = 'product'
ORDER  BY sort_order;


-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- SECTION 1 — the five templates
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

BEGIN;

DO $$
